> >
> I was misled by Documentation/SubmittingPatches.
> Patch: remove the bootsect_helper code and a comment fix also.


update Documentation/SubmittingPatches to current patch level,
change references from 2.4 to 2.6 and more important suggest the
users home dir for his patching and source building 
(fhs compliance).
change tar.gz in bz2 use.

please speak up if you prefer variable names MYTREE versus SRCTREE,
tried to be consistent in choice.

a++ maks



--- linux-2.6.6/Documentation/SubmittingPatches	2004-04-26 11:16:30.000000000 +0200
+++ linux-2.6.6-maks/Documentation/SubmittingPatches	2004-04-26 13:14:41.000000000 +0200
@@ -35,25 +35,28 @@ not in any lower subdirectory.
 
 To create a patch for a single file, it is often sufficient to do:
 
-	SRCTREE= /devel/linux-2.4
+	SRCTREE= ~/src/linux-2.6.x
+	MYTREE=  ~/src/linux-2.6.x-foo
 	MYFILE=  drivers/net/mydriver.c
 
-	cd $SRCTREE
-	cp $MYFILE $MYFILE.orig
+	cd ~/src
+	cp -a $SRCTREE $MYTREE
+	cd $MYTREE
 	vi $MYFILE	# make your change
-	diff -up $MYFILE.orig $MYFILE > /tmp/patch
+	cd ..
+	diff -up $SRCTREE/$MYFILE $MYTREE/$MYFILE > /tmp/patch
 
 To create a patch for multiple files, you should unpack a "vanilla",
 or unmodified kernel source tree, and generate a diff against your
 own source tree.  For example:
 
-	MYSRC= /devel/linux-2.4
+	MYTREE= ~/src/linux-2.6.x-foo
 
-	tar xvfz linux-2.4.0-test11.tar.gz
-	mv linux linux-vanilla
+	cd ~/src
+	tar xvfj linux-2.6.6.bz2
+	mv linux-2.6.6 linux-vanilla
 	wget http://www.moses.uklinux.net/patches/dontdiff
-	diff -uprN -X dontdiff linux-vanilla $MYSRC > /tmp/patch
-	rm -f dontdiff
+	diff -uprN -X dontdiff linux-vanilla $MYTREE > /tmp/patch
 
 "dontdiff" is a list of files which are generated by the kernel during
 the build process, and should be ignored in any diff(1)-generated


